---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="live-count — rhocfsandbox" active="demos">
  <h1><span class="prompt">demo: durable objects live viewer count</span></h1>
  <p class="dim">
    open this page in multiple tabs (or share the url with a friend). every
    open tab holds a websocket to the same durable object, which broadcasts
    the current connection count to all listeners in real time.
  </p>

  <div class="hero-count" id="count">–</div>
  <div class="hero-label">viewers connected right now</div>

  <div class="log-out" id="log-out"><span class="dim">connecting…</span></div>

  <h2># how it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>page opens a websocket to <code>wss://site-livecount-do.rhomanie-demo-account.workers.dev/ws</code></li>
    <li>the worker forwards the upgrade to a durable object stub keyed by <code>global</code></li>
    <li>the DO accepts the ws, adds it to an in-memory <code>Set</code>, and broadcasts <code>{`{count}`}</code> to all sessions</li>
    <li>on <code>close</code>/<code>error</code>, the DO removes the session and rebroadcasts</li>
    <li>every visitor talks to the same DO instance because the id is derived from a fixed name</li>
  </ol>

  <h2># why not just workers?</h2>
  <p class="dim">
    workers are stateless. two requests can land on different workers with
    no shared memory. durable objects give you a single-threaded actor with
    strongly consistent in-memory state, pinned to one region. perfect for
    coordination like counters, chat rooms, game lobbies, presence.
  </p>

  <style>
    .hero-count { font-size: 4rem; color: #00d4ff; font-weight: bold; text-align: center; margin: 1rem 0; }
    .hero-label { text-align: center; color: #888; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  </style>

  <script>
    const countEl = document.getElementById('count');
    const out = document.getElementById('log-out');
    let ws;
    function connect() {
      const url = 'wss://site-livecount-do.rhomanie-demo-account.workers.dev/ws';
      out.innerHTML = `<span class="dim">→ ${url}</span>`;
      ws = new WebSocket(url);
      ws.onopen = () => { out.innerHTML += `<div class="line ok">connected. waiting for count…</div>`; };
      ws.onmessage = (e) => {
        try {
          const j = JSON.parse(e.data);
          if (j.type === 'count') countEl.textContent = j.count;
        } catch {}
      };
      ws.onclose = () => { out.innerHTML += `<div class="line warn">closed. reconnecting in 2s…</div>`; setTimeout(connect, 2000); };
      ws.onerror = (e) => { out.innerHTML += `<div class="line err">error</div>`; };
    }
    connect();
  </script>
</Layout>
